meta {
  name: 27 Print All Reports
  type: http
  seq: 27
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/lab-reports/order/{{orderId}}/print-all
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{businessAccessToken}}
}

body:json {
  {}
}

tests {
  test("200 or 201 with a PDF", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.be.oneOf([200, 201]);
    expect(res.getHeader("content-type")).to.include("application/pdf");
  });
}

docs {
  Print All (order-console's "Lab All Report" action). Streams the rendered PDF
  directly (`Content-Type: application/pdf`). Declared before the `:id` routes
  on the controller (same convention as `tat/summary`), so `order` in the URL is
  never captured as a report id.

  ## Two render paths (by selected template type)

  - **`lab_all_report` template →** the whole order is rendered as ONE
    continuous document by the **Latte engine** (`LatteReportRenderService`).
    The template iterates every test itself (`{foreach $report_tests->tests as
    $r}`) and manages its own per-test page headers/breaks. See
    `buildAllReportsContext` in `lab-report.service.ts`.
  - **any other template (or none) →** each report is rendered as its own
    single-report PDF (same path as `26 Print Report`) and the PDFs are merged
    with `pdf-lib` — the legacy fallback.

  ## Body

  - `templateId` — optional. When it is a `lab_all_report` template the Latte
    path is used; otherwise the per-report merge runs. Omit to use the tenant's
    single active `lab_report` template for the merge fallback.
  - `orderItemIds` — optional array; restrict to these order items' reports.

  ## Latte render context (`lab_all_report`)

  - `report_tests.tests[]` — one entry per test/report on the order, each with
    `body_html` (its result table + content sections, built from the
    single-report context), `sample_collected_date`, `sample_received_date`,
    `report_prepared_on` (that report's own approval time,
    `DD-MM-YYYY hh:mm AM/PM`), `test_file_attachment` (that test's own File+
    PDF(s) as `<embed>` markup: `{$report->test_file_attachment|noescape}`),
    `lab_test_id`, and `tests[].display_test_sample`.
  - `report_tests.groups[]` — the department-grouped section; currently empty
    (all reports flow through `tests[]`, which templates iterate identically).
  - `header_fields` — shared order/patient header block: `patient.{salutation,
    full_name}`, `client_age`, `client_gender` (M/F), `client_phone`,
    `client_uhid`, `order_code`, `external_order_id`, `sample_source_label`,
    `refer_by_name`, `referring_panel_name`, `order_date_time`, `order_id_barcode`.
    `order_code` is the Order ID (`Order.orderCode`, e.g. `ORD-00114`); it is
    also aliased at the context root so the bare `{order_code}` tag works.
    `client_age` is one unit (0-29 days → Days, under 1 year → Months, else
    Years); `order_date_time` is the typed order time, else the order's
    `createdAt` in the tenant timezone; `order_id_barcode` is a Code 128
    barcode image src (`data:` URI) of the Order ID (`Order.orderCode`).

  ## Resolution & errors

  - 404 `ORDER_REPORTS_NOT_FOUND` — the order (or the selected items) has no
    `LabReport` rows (wrong id, or no item has reached ACCEPTED yet).
  - 404 `NO_ACTIVE_PRINT_TEMPLATE` / 409 `AMBIGUOUS_PRINT_TEMPLATE` — merge
    fallback only, when no `templateId` is given and zero / multiple active
    `lab_report` templates exist.

  ## Notes sections left out of the print

  The five Notes tags (`{useful_for}`, `{interpretation}`, `{limitations}`,
  `{remarks}`, `{references}`) are blank for any section the technician left out
  of each report (`PATCH /lab-reports/:id/note-print-sections`). It applies only
  while the matching Branch Setting (Branch Admin > Branch Settings > Technician Settings > Report Printing) is on (Lab Panel switch for a report on a
  panel order line, Lab Test switch otherwise, read from the branch that holds the
  report); while it is off every section prints. A flat template hides the heading
  and box of a blank section by wrapping the block in `{{#if useful_for}}...{{/if}}`
  (see docs/PRINT-TEMPLATES.md); a Latte template uses `{if $test->useful_for}`.

  ## Lab panel tags

  Each test carries `is_lab_panel`, `lab_panel_name` and `lab_panel_code`: in a Latte
  all-reports template `{if $report->is_lab_panel}...{/if}` per test (a real
  boolean); in a flat `{{#each reports}}` template `{{#if is_lab_panel}}...{{/if}}`
  inside the block decides per test (the text `Yes` or empty). They are empty at the
  top level, because whether a test is in a panel is not a property of the whole
  order. Each test's own body (`body_html`) also has the tags (see Print Report).
}
